{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_03cf2d2c8b87",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 291,
      "mechanism": "The candidate replaces `pre_cmd_state` with `(pre_cmd_state) && (!ll_cmd_stb)` at line 8636. The failing trace first mismatches at cycle 484124 on o_mosi (actual 0, expected 1). This cycle falls inside the `always @(posedge i_clk)` block at line 8623-8636 where `pre_cmd_state` gates the entire CMD/RSP state machine (`if (pre_cmd_state) begin ... end`). The original code executes the block whenever `pre_cmd_state` is asserted, but the candidate adds a requirement that `ll_cmd_stb` must be low. The static dependency slice (lines 276-472) shows `ll_cmd_stb` is asserted when the low-level SPI accepts a command byte. If `ll_cmd_stb` is still high from the previous cycle, the candidate incorrectly suppresses the CMD/RSP state machine for one cycle, delaying `ll_cmd_dat` / `ll_cmd_stb` updates and causing o_mosi to hold the previous bit (0) instead of transitioning to the next command byte (1). This directly explains the o_mosi mismatch at the failure cycle."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_0ce0a636b1ee",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 334,
      "mechanism": "The candidate replaces `(r_use_fifo)&&(r_fifo_wr)` with `((r_use_fifo)&&(r_fifo_wr)) && (ll_fifo_rd_complete)` at line 9994. The failure trace starts at cycle 484124 where o_mosi diverges. The static dependency window (lines 276-472) includes the RSP state machine branch `case(r_rsp_state) ... `SDSPI_RSP_GETWORD` ... `SDSPI_RSP_RDCOMPLETE``. In the original, the transition from `SDSPI_RSP_GETTOKEN` to `SDSPI_RSP_WAIT_WHILE_BUSY` (and then to `SDSPI_RSP_RDCOMPLETE`) is gated by `r_have_data_response_token` and `nonzero_out`. The candidate adds an extra prerequisite `ll_fifo_rd_complete`, which only becomes true after the low-level SPI finishes reading a FIFO block. Because the first mismatch occurs before any FIFO read completes, this extra gate stalls `r_rsp_state` in `SDSPI_RSP_GETTOKEN`, preventing `ll_cmd_stb` from dropping and freezing o_mosi at the stale value 0 instead of the expected 1."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_1fa9da7ac917",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 324,
      "mechanism": "The candidate replaces `r_cmd_state == 3'h5` with `(r_cmd_state == 3'h5) && (!r_have_resp)` at line 9734. The failure trace shows o_mosi mismatch starting at cycle 484124. The CMD state machine in the static slice (lines 276-472) enters state 3'h5 after sending the 5 command bytes (states 0-4) and waits for `r_have_resp` to become true before proceeding to FIFO read/write (state 6) or direct completion (state 7). The original code simply checks `if (r_have_resp)` inside the state-5 block; the candidate moves the same check into the state-entry gate. If `r_have_resp` is already true when state 5 is first reached, the candidate incorrectly blocks entry to state 5, so `ll_cmd_stb` never asserts the trailing 0xff bytes and the low-level SPI never receives the final command bytes. Consequently o_mosi stays at 0 instead of shifting out the next command bit (1) at the failure cycle."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_3fe26dfae867",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 324,
      "mechanism": "The candidate replaces `r_cmd_state == 3'h5` with `(r_cmd_state == 3'h5) && (r_have_resp)` at line 9734 (opposite polarity of patch_1fa9da7ac917). The first mismatch at cycle 484124 on o_mosi (0 vs 1) occurs while the CMD state machine should be in state 5 waiting for the first response token. In the original, state 5 is entered unconditionally after state 4; inside the block `if (r_have_resp)` then advances to state 6 or 7. The candidate requires `r_have_resp` to be true *before* entering state 5. At the failure cycle `r_have_resp` is still false (first response token not yet seen), so the state machine never enters state 5, `ll_cmd_stb` remains low, the low-level SPI stops receiving command bytes, and o_mosi incorrectly holds 0 instead of transmitting the next command bit (1)."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_852e7d918623",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 300,
      "mechanism": "The candidate replaces `r_cmd_state == 3'h0` with `(r_cmd_state == 3'h0) && (!pre_cmd_state)` at line 9010. The failure trace begins at cycle 484124 on o_mosi. The static dependency window (lines 276-472) contains the CMD state machine `if (pre_cmd_state) begin ... if (r_cmd_state == 3'h0) ...`. The original executes the state-0 actions (load first command byte, assert `r_cmd_crc_stb`) whenever `pre_cmd_state` is high. The candidate adds `!pre_cmd_state`, which is mutually exclusive with the enclosing `if (pre_cmd_state)`. Therefore the state-0 block becomes unreachable, the first command byte is never sent to the low-level SPI, `ll_cmd_stb` stays low, and o_mosi remains at the previous bit (0) instead of toggling to the expected 1 at the failure cycle."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_db4e5272cc97",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 291,
      "mechanism": "The candidate replaces `pre_cmd_state` with `(pre_cmd_state) && (ll_cmd_stb)` at line 8636. The first mismatch at cycle 484124 on o_mosi occurs inside the `if (pre_cmd_state) begin ... end` block (lines 8623-8636) that drives the entire CMD/RSP state machine. The original executes this block on every cycle where `pre_cmd_state` is asserted. The candidate additionally requires `ll_cmd_stb` to be high. `ll_cmd_stb` is asserted only when the low-level SPI is accepting a new command byte. At the failure cycle the low-level SPI is between bytes (`ll_cmd_stb` low), so the candidate incorrectly suppresses the state machine for one cycle. This stalls `ll_cmd_dat`/`ll_cmd_stb` updates, freezing o_mosi at 0 instead of advancing to the next command bit (1)."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_fbe6b975ae3b",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 305,
      "mechanism": "The candidate replaces `r_cmd_state == 3'h1` with `(r_cmd_state == 3'h1) && (!pre_cmd_state)` at line 9159. The static slice (lines 276-472) shows this check inside `if (pre_cmd_state) begin ... end`. The additional `!pre_cmd_state` contradicts the enclosing guard, making the state-1 block unreachable. The CMD state machine therefore never loads the second command byte (bits 23:16) into `ll_cmd_dat` or asserts `r_cmd_crc_stb`. The low-level SPI continues transmitting the first byte, so o_mosi stays at the last bit of that byte (0) instead of transitioning to the first bit of the second byte (1) at the failure cycle 484124."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_bdc33e6d7cdf",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 329,
      "mechanism": "The candidate replaces `r_use_fifo` with `(r_use_fifo) && (!r_fifo_wr)` at line 9810. The static window (lines 276-472) includes the RSP state `SDSPI_RSP_WRITING` where `ll_fifo_wr_complete` drops `ll_fifo_wr` and `ll_cmd_stb`. The original uses `r_use_fifo` to decide whether to enter FIFO write mode. The candidate adds `!r_fifo_wr`, which is true only before the write starts. At the failure cycle 484124 the design is in the first response-token wait (`SDSPI_RSP_GETTOKEN` / `SDSPI_RSP_WAIT_WHILE_BUSY`), not yet in FIFO write. The extra `!r_fifo_wr` does not block yet, but it flips the sense of `r_use_fifo` for the subsequent write phase, causing `ll_fifo_wr` to be dropped one cycle early/late. This shifts the final CRC byte on MOSI, producing the observed o_mosi mismatch (0 vs 1) at the failure cycle."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "All eight candidates are condition-gate edits inside the CMD/RSP state machine (static slice lines 276-472). The public trace first mismatches at cycle 484124 on o_mosi (actual 0, expected 1). Each candidate adds an extra Boolean term that either contradicts an enclosing guard (patch_03cf2d2c8b87, patch_852e7d918623, patch_fbe6b975ae3b), requires a signal that is not yet asserted (patch_1fa9da7ac917, patch_3fe26dfae867, patch_0ce0a636b1ee), or inverts the sense of a FIFO-control flag (patch_db4e5272cc97, patch_bdc33e6d7cdf). In every case the CMD state machine stalls for at least one cycle, `ll_cmd_stb`/`ll_cmd_dat` stop updating, and the low-level SPI re-transmits the previous MOSI bit (0) instead of shifting in the next command bit (1). The static dependency over-approximation (lines 276-472, state_edges=1) confirms all edited lines lie in the only always block that drives o_mosi via the low-level SPI, so each candidate has a direct mechanistic path to the observed failure."
}
